{
 "id": "2025-baniadam-streetscape",
 "name": "Streetscape Vegetation Choice with Restorative Reasons",
 "description": "A choice experiment that asks people not only which design they prefer but also why, in terms of restoration. Baniadam et al. (2025, PLOS ONE) built two virtual streets in Nantes in which only one design feature changes at a time. In 30 tasks, 57 participants in a VR headset switched between two or three versions of the same scene, chose the one they preferred, and ticked one or more reasons based on the four parts of Attention Restoration Theory: \"I chose this option because it is… more interesting or fascinating\" (fascination), \"…more orderly or more coherent\" (coherence), \"…more effective to get away from daily routine\" (being away), \"…more suited to my personality\" (compatibility). Participants imagined that the place is on a route they walk every day and is not their home. Results: no fence was preferred in every fence task; shorter fences and fences with greenery were preferred, mostly for fascination; high metal fences were picked less often and, when picked, for coherence; trees were the most preferred vegetation (fascination), while bushes and grass were chosen for coherence. The page shows one version at a time with \"Option 1 of 2\" as in the headset; participants must look at every option before choosing, can flip as often as they like (the number of switches is recorded), and then tick reasons. Records: choice (key = task id as in the paper's Table 2, value = chosen file, shown = all options) and reasons (per task: reasons ticked and number of views). Analysis as in the paper: per task, a binomial test of the most chosen option and of the most ticked reason. The paper's raw choice data are open (S1 and S2 Data, XLSX), so new samples can be compared directly. Images: the paper's Figs 2–3 (CC BY 4.0), 8 fence versions (high/short × metal/wood × with/without greenery) and 6 vegetation versions. Differences from the original study: (1) the template has the 10 tasks whose options are all printed in the paper: tasks 9–16 (fence height, material, greenery) and tasks 17 and 24 (vegetation type and combination); tasks 1–8 need a \"no fence\" image and tasks 18–23 and 25–30 need diversity and arrangement versions that the paper does not show; add your own renderings to run them; (2) participants see still images on a screen instead of walking in VR; (3) the option order within a task is randomised (not reported in the paper); tasks are in random order as in the paper; (4) the paper also asked about VR and 3D game experience; the template asks only age and gender.",
